title: Investigate how beads is actually used in a downstream project
effort: M
area: format
created: 2026-09-17T06:54:13Z
updated: 2026-09-18T20:37:59Z
handoff:

The downstream project is the most mature one on beads, so it is the
only evidence available about which bd concepts are load-bearing in
practice rather than in bd's feature list. 6 cannot be planned honestly
without it.

What is there, from a shallow look:

  .beads/issues.jsonl        342 issues
  .beads/interactions.jsonl  455 interactions
  .beads/embeddeddolt/       an embedded Dolt database
  .beads/                    94M total

The 94M is worth stating plainly, since pidly's premise is that the
repository is the interface: the whole of pidly's own tracker is 37 tickets
of plain text, and the comparison belongs in the design rather than in a
sales pitch.

An issue carries: _type, assignee, close_reason, closed_at, comment_count,
created_at, created_by, dependency_count, dependent_count, description, id,
issue_type, owner, priority, started_at, status, title, updated_at.

Questions to answer from the data, not from opinion:

- Which fields are actually populated across the 342, and how often. A field
  every issue leaves at its default is not a requirement pidly has to meet.
- assignee, owner and created_by are three identities. Are they ever
  different from each other? pidly has one, and it means "who holds it now".
- status: how many distinct values are used, and do any of them mean
  something other than open or closed? pidly has two, by location.
- priority: is it used, and is it used to sort, or set once and ignored?
  That is the trigger 34 is waiting on, and this is the place to test it.
- issue_type: what values, and would they be a project field, a parent
  ticket, or nothing.
- dependency_count and dependent_count are derived. Is the graph shallow
  enough that pidly's needs: covers it, and are there relation types beyond
  blocking — bd has relates-to, duplicates, supersedes, discovered-from,
  which the design rules out on principle 3.
- close_reason and the 455 interactions: what is in them, and where would
  that land in pidly — the body, handoff, or nowhere. This is the one that
  could argue for something pidly does not have.
- Are issues ever reopened, and are they ever re-parented.

Then: what a migration would produce. 342 tickets is small enough to convert
in a script — id to number, title to title, status to location, assignee to
claim, dependencies to needs, description plus comments to the body — and
the interesting part is what has no home, not what maps cleanly.

Done when the answers are written into this ticket's body, 6 can be planned
against them, and anything that argues for a change in pidly is its own
ticket rather than a paragraph here.


Answered 2026-09-18, from the downstream project's .beads. Every number below is counted from
the files, not from bd's documentation.

FIRST, THE FINDING THAT CHANGES 46: the export is stale and incomplete.

  export-state.json  last export 2026-08-02, issues: 332
  issues.jsonl       332 issues, latest updated_at 2026-08-02T17:21:38
  interactions.jsonl 455 entries, latest 2026-09-17T00:18:29

interactions.jsonl kept logging for six weeks after issues.jsonl froze — 144
entries after the last export — and references 100 issue ids that are not in
issues.jsonl at all. Every one of those 100 first appears after 2026-08-02,
so they are issues created since the export, not deletions. The project really has
around 432 issues; the file a migration would read shows 332.

So .beads/*.jsonl is a snapshot someone has to remember to refresh, and this
one was not refreshed for six weeks of daily work. A migration that reads it
silently drops a quarter of the tracker. Recorded on 46.

Worth saying plainly, since the premise is that the repository is the
interface: .beads is 94M — 56M embedded Dolt, 38M backup — against 760K of
JSONL that is itself stale. pidly's whole tracker, 46 tickets, is 120K of
text with no database to fall behind.

THE QUESTIONS, IN ORDER.

Which fields are populated. 100%: id, title, description, status,
issue_type, owner, created_at, created_by, updated_at. Then priority 99%,
closed_at and close_reason 83%, assignee and started_at 65%, notes 38%,
dependencies 34%, acceptance_criteria 21%, labels 9%, design 1%.

Three identities, one person. assignee, owner and created_by take exactly two
combinations across 332 issues, and both are the same human: assignee is
either ShannonTully or empty, owner is always the same address, created_by is
always ShannonTully. assignee is set on exactly the 217 issues that have
started_at, and no issue is started-but-open — so assignee does not mean "who
holds it", it means "this was worked, and is finished". pidly's claim: is the
first meaning and git already records the second. Nothing here argues for a
second identity field.

status has four values, and two of them are rare: 278 closed, 51 open, 2
deferred, 1 in_progress. deferred maps to pidly's defer:. in_progress is one
issue in sixteen months, and it is the state pidly deliberately does not
have — a claim naming a process nobody can check. Import as unclaimed.

priority is set on 331 issues and 218 of them sit at 2, the default. In 455
logged changes it was edited exactly once, on 2026-09-07, from 2 to 1. That
is the measurement 34 has been waiting for and it argues the other way: a
field nobody re-ranks is not a sort key, and promoting it to a fourth header
line would make every ticket carry a line that one ticket in 332 has ever
used. Noted on 34.

issue_type has six values but three carry it: task 131, feature 96, bug 93,
then epic 4, decision 4, chore 4. A project field with a match: pattern is
the honest home if a project wants it. epic is parentage, which pidly spells
as a directory.

labels is the strongest negative result here. 33 issues carry labels, and
across all of them there is exactly one distinct label: feedback-2026-07-22,
a dated batch marker. Sixteen months of use produced no taxonomy — one
throwaway tag for one afternoon's triage. 35 defers set-valued fields until
someone wants to filter across a dimension that is not already a field, and
this is evidence the trigger has not fired even on the mature project. Noted
on 35.

Dependencies: three edge types in use, and bd's wider vocabulary is unused.
parent-child 83, blocks 76, blocked-by 7, and zero of relates-to, duplicates,
supersedes or discovered-from. blocks and blocked-by are the same relation
written from either end, which is pidly's needs: with the arrow fixed. The
design ruled the rest out on principle 3 and the corpus never needed them.

The graph is shallow. 83 blocking edges over 42 issues: 29 have one
dependency, 8 have two, and the tail is 3, 4, 6, 12 and 13. Parentage is 83
edges over 5 parents — two large ones with 42 and 32 children, three with 5,
3 and 1 — nesting never goes deeper than two levels, and no child has more
than one parent. That last one matters: pidly puts a child inside its parent's
directory, so a child with two parents would have nowhere to live. In sixteen
months it never happened.

close_reason is mostly empty ceremony: 171 of 278 are the literal string
"Closed". The other 107 are real prose, often naming a commit — "Implemented
in 7abe4f4", "Investigated (2026-07-22): not a bug". Those belong in the
body, appended under a heading, because that is where pidly already puts what
was learned.

The 455 interactions are not a discussion log. Every one is kind
field_change, 454 of them status and 1 priority. There are no comments, no
attachments and no narrative — comment_count is 0 on all 332 issues. So the
thing that looked like it might argue for something pidly lacks turns out to
be a status history, which pidly gets from git for free and more honestly: a
close is a commit that moves a directory. Nothing here needs a new pidly
feature.

Reopening never happened: no issue has closed_at with a non-closed status,
and no issue is started but open. Re-parenting cannot be seen in the export
at all, since only the current edge is stored — one more thing the snapshot
cannot tell you and git could.

The 10 memory records are ADR-shaped prose keyed by slug — a key/value store
bolted to the tracker. They are documents, not tickets, and they belong in
the repository as documents. An import should report and skip them.

WHAT THIS MEANS FOR 6. The bd concepts that are load-bearing are: title,
description, open/closed, a blocking edge, a parent, and timestamps. pidly
has all six. The concepts that exist and are not load-bearing are priority,
labels, the extra relation types, three identities, comments, and the
interaction log. 6 can be planned on the first list, and the second list is
the argument for the migration dropping things loudly rather than growing
pidly to receive them.

Nothing here argues for a change to pidly. The two things it does argue for
are triggers NOT firing, noted on 34 and 35, and one constraint on 46.
